		{
			"id": "111982242706621581",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://c.im/@cwilcox808/111982242687814260",
			"author": {
				"name": "Curtis Wilcox",
				"url": "https://c.im/@cwilcox808",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/611/015/490/799/806/original/79bff32e81a0f372.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2024-02-23T18:36:59.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <br>I assume the job is a two-way street but not a public one: publicly putting out what the company wants developers to know, privately taking in and passing on what developers want the company to know. </p><p>On the outside, if they do the latter part well, you might see results from it. Often even when done well, you won't see results because decision-makers won't listen or stick to other, competing interests. They don't have the power to do more.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
