			},
			"date": "2024-02-24T00:41:06.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@patrick_h_lauke\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>patrick_h_lauke</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I was a Mac developer before I was a web developer (before there WAS a web). Then a Mac developer of web tools, and for 20 years+ no longer a Mac developer. </p><p>more than 30 years ago someone I knew who'd been around the block already by then observed he felt with Apple as a developer you were \"fuel for their rocket not a passenger on it\" It stuck with me.</p><p>And the way Apple treated the devs who stick with them in the dark days of MacOS 8/9 when X came along…</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
